.TH ZZ9K_PROBE 1M "15 November 2026" "amix-z3660rtg" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
zz9k_probe \- say which board is behind the Z3660/ZZ9000 RTG driver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zz9k_probe z3660
.br
.B zz9k_probe zz9000
.RB [ 0 | 1 ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zz9k_probe
is the probe of the RTG driver's card files in
.IR /etc/display.d :
the display start-up runs it to learn whether the card a file describes is
there (see the PROBES section of
.BR display.d (4)).
The Z3660's onboard RTG and an MNT ZZ9000 card share the driver and its
node names, so whether a node opens cannot tell them apart.
.PP
For units 0 and 1 it opens the information node,
.I /dev/zz9000i
or
.IR /dev/zz9001i ,
read-only, asks the driver for the unit's board identity with one
.B ZZ9KIOC_GETINFO
and closes it.
The driver answers from what it found when it loaded; nothing is written to
any board and the main nodes are never opened.
A unit whose node does not open, or for which the driver has no board, is
not there.
Opening the information node loads the driver's module when it is not
loaded yet, as any open of the node does.
.TP
.B z3660
Exit 0 when unit 0 is the Z3660's RTG, and print
.RS
.nf
node /dev/zz9000
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B zz9000
Take the first unit, 0 then 1, that is a ZZ9000 card, Zorro II or Zorro
III, print its node,
.RS
.nf
node /dev/zz9001
.fi
.RE
.IP
and exit 0.
For a card in a Zorro III slot it also prints the modes it takes at 16
bits, the card file's list and 1920x1080:
.RS
.nf
modes 1024x768 640x480 800x600 1280x720 1280x1024 1920x800 1920x1080
.fi
.RE
.IP
A Zorro II card's framebuffer (its 4 MB window less the register page and
the firmware's tail, 3.8 MB) holds 1920x800 at 16 bits but not 1920x1080.
.TP
.BR "zz9000 0" ", " "zz9000 1"
The same for that unit only: exit 0 and print its node (and, in a Zorro
III slot, its modes) when that unit is a ZZ9000 card; otherwise exit 1 with
no output.
Only that unit's information node is opened.
This is for card files, which cannot pass the node through and so need one
file per unit.
.PP
It prints nothing else and never writes to its standard error unless the
arguments are wrong.
It returns at once, and is safe to run as any user, with no board fitted
and inside a
.BR chroot (1M).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the board asked for is there; 1 when it is not (no such unit, no
node, no driver, another board); 2 for a usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP 20
.I /dev/zz9000i
unit 0's information node (character 49, minor 128, mode 0444)
.TP
.I /dev/zz9001i
unit 1's (character 49, minor 129, mode 0444)
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR display.d (4),
.BR xstart (1),
.BR zz9000 (7)
